WEBVTT

1
-1:59:59.840 --> 00:00:06.260
I was recently learning a new-to-me lace
carriage, and the manual reminded me

2
00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:10.900
of what I consider
to be the worst cast on ever.

3
00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.240
I understand that with a lace carriage,

4
00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:17.020
I can't pull needles out
past the B position.

5
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:23.180
Most of the closed cast on methods that I
use pull the needles out further.

6
00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:29.460
But this sloppy, open cast on is
often taught to new knitters.

7
00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.380
No wonder they get discouraged.

8
00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:35.220
Here are the steps.
I'm demonstrating

9
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:41.180
this on a Silver Reed machine with my lace
carriage, but this method is often

10
00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:44.660
recommended on other
machines with gate pegs.

11
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.800
I have my lace carriage in place
and it's set for plain knitting.

12
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:55.260
Align all the needles to B position
and then thread up the carriage.

13
00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.220
Knit across.

14
00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:03.960
You'll see open loops
across all the needles.

15
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.020
Next, run a strand of ravel cord behind

16
00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:16.480
the gate pegs and in front of the needle
hooks.

17
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.340
The object here is to hold those
loose loops down with the ravel cord.

18
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:29.570
Hold the tail of the working yarn
and the tails of the ravel cord securely.

19
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.340
Pass the carriage and knit one row.

20
00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:41.280
If all the stitches knit correctly,
continue knitting four or five rows.

21
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:48.920
Release the knitting
by slipping the ravel cord out.

22
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.490
Immediately, you see a problem when
I hang weights on the edge stitches.

23
00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:02.960
The stitches are not secure.

24
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.660
As a matter of fact, the stitches will
ravel, and because of all those open

25
00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:14.420
stitches, the edge of your
knitting is going to be a mess.

26
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:20.940
I have never understood why anyone
would recommend or use this method.

27
00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.140
Why not simply work a nice closed cast

28
00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:29.540
on with your regular knitting carriage,
and then switch to the lace carriage?

29
00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:34.300
If you don't have more than one cast
on method in your bag of tricks, I suggest

30
00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:38.780
you learn at least two or three
to use for different situations.

31
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.600
But please stay away from this worst
cast on ever.

